ML_PAGE("l9-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l9\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 9 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Chapter 9 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 30 multiple-choice questions sit inside the sections they belong to, with the answer, the logic and the exam trap hidden until you reveal them.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>MCQs in the notes</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Exam traps explained</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Difficult-mode questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l9\" data-quiz=\"9\">Start Chapter 9 simulator</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l9\" id=\"l9p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">2 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A - PRACTICE QUESTIONS</li><li data-full=\"Questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l9p-s2\" title=\"Questions\">Practice questions (Q1-Q30)</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B - REVISION AND ERROR DIAGNOSIS</li><li data-full=\"Quick revision and error diagnosis\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l9p-s4\" title=\"Quick revision and error diagnosis\">Quick revision and error diagnosis</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l9\" open><summary><div><strong>Chapter 9 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Exam strategy, practice questions, formula sheets and review - section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>2 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2>Chapter 9 - Market Efficiency and Behavioral Finance: MCQ Practice</h2>\n<p><strong>30 questions · suggested time: 45 minutes · practice target: 80%.</strong> These are illustrative study targets, not official exam requirements. Choose one best answer. Attempt the questions before reading the separate key.</p>\n<p>A useful reasoning sequence is: <strong>What information was available? → What does the price already reflect? → What alternative explanation fits? → Is a trading signal actually dependable after costs and risk?</strong></p><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l9p-d1\">PART A - PRACTICE QUESTIONS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l9p-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>A company announces higher earnings, but investors expected an even larger increase. Its share price falls. What is the most defensible explanation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A decline proves all market prices are random errors.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A positive earnings increase guarantees a positive price change.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The market did not receive any information.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The announcement may be disappointing relative to expectations already reflected in the price.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Prices react to news relative to expectations; good reported growth may still disappoint investors.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Good news in absolute terms is not necessarily a positive surprise.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>In an efficient market, which claim is the best interpretation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Investors cannot lose money.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Prices rapidly reflect the relevant information specified by the efficiency form; abnormal risk-adjusted profits are difficult to earn consistently from that information.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Every security earns the same return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Prices never change when news arrives.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Efficiency concerns information reflected in price and the difficulty of consistently earning abnormal risk-adjusted returns from it.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Efficient does not mean risk-free or error-free at every instant.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>Weak-form market efficiency concerns which information set?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Only undisclosed management plans.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Material private information only.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Past trading information such as historical prices and volume.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> All future news before it occurs.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Weak-form efficiency concerns information in past trading data, including price histories.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Private information belongs to the stronger information set.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>If the semi-strong form holds, which approach should not consistently earn abnormal risk-adjusted returns using its specified information alone?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Trading after a public earnings announcement using only that already-public information.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Purchasing a diversified portfolio for long-term goals.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Collecting dividends.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Earning a positive nominal return when markets rise.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Semi-strong efficiency implies that public information is rapidly incorporated, limiting consistent abnormal returns from trading on it alone.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Normal positive returns and dividends can still occur.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>Which information set is included in the strong form of the efficient-market hypothesis?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Public and private information.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Public information only, excluding past prices.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Past prices only.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Future random events known with certainty.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Strong-form efficiency includes public and private information.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The hypothesis does not mean that unknown future events are already known.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>Semi-strong efficiency is supported for a given market. What follows about the nested information sets?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It automatically proves strong-form efficiency.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Public information includes past trading data, so the semi-strong form encompasses the weak form.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Insider information becomes public by definition.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Every individual forecast is correct.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Historical prices are public, so semi-strong efficiency includes the weak information set; it does not prove the strong form.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Nested forms do not imply the reverse conclusion.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>Why might stock prices exhibit unpredictable short-run changes in an efficient market?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Prices must remain constant forever.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> News never changes values.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Unexpected information, rather than a predictable pattern known in advance, can move prices.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Firms do not publish financial statements.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>If predictable information is already incorporated, new unexpected information may drive subsequent price changes.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Unpredictable changes do not mean prices are unrelated to value or news.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>An investor identifies an apparent January return pattern in a short historical sample. What is the best next step?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Assume it must persist and ignore costs.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Assume any positive average is an abnormal risk-adjusted return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Conclude all markets are perfectly inefficient.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Test its robustness out of sample and consider risk, chance, data selection and trading costs.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A short sample can reflect chance or selection; risk adjustment, realistic costs and out-of-sample testing matter.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A historical pattern is not by itself a durable opportunity.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>Small-company stocks have historically outperformed in one sample. Which explanation must be considered before calling the difference a free profit?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Small companies are risk-free by definition.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> They may have greater or differently measured risk, and sample choice may matter.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Any past return difference is guaranteed to continue.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Market capitalization is unrelated to company size.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Risk exposure, model choice and sample selection are potential explanations for observed outperformance.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Higher realized returns do not automatically mean risk-free abnormal returns.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>After unexpectedly good earnings are announced, prices continue drifting upward over subsequent weeks. This pattern is called:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Post-earnings-announcement drift.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A stock split.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A dividend reinvestment plan.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> An immediate bond redemption.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Continuing price adjustment after an earnings announcement is post-earnings-announcement drift.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A pattern does not establish profitability after costs and risk adjustment.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>Which statement best describes a market anomaly?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Evidence that investors can ignore transaction costs.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A guaranteed trading profit in every future period.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A pattern apparently inconsistent with a particular model or efficiency prediction, requiring further investigation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Proof that all securities have the same risk.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>An anomaly is judged relative to a model or hypothesis and may have several explanations.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Observation is not proof of a reliable future trade.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>An investor assigns a 95% chance of correctly forecasting a stock's next move despite repeated forecasting errors and takes a very large position. Which bias is most directly illustrated?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Duration matching.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Overconfidence.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Currency conversion.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Loss aversion.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Overconfidence means excessive certainty about one's knowledge or forecasting ability.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Confidence is not the same as evidence of accuracy.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>An investor credits profitable trades entirely to skill and blames every loss on bad luck or other people. Which bias is most directly illustrated?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Diversification.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Market breadth.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Liquidity preference.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Self-attribution bias.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Self-attribution credits oneself for successes and external factors for failures.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A profitable trade alone does not isolate skill from chance.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>A shareholder refuses to consider selling a losing stock solely because selling would make the loss feel real, despite changed fundamentals. Which bias is most relevant?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Efficient diversification.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Market capitalization.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Interest coverage.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Loss aversion.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Loss aversion can make realizing a loss emotionally difficult; selling is not always correct, but decisions should reflect current evidence.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The original purchase price is not by itself a reason to hold.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>A trader assumes a company is certain to keep growing because its last two quarters were excellent, ignoring the small sample and industry cycles. This is most directly:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A bond call provision.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> An advance-decline measure.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Representativeness.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> An inventory turnover ratio.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Representativeness can lead to strong conclusions from a small sample perceived as typical.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Two good quarters do not establish a permanent growth rate.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>An investor assesses a new stock in isolation and ignores its large correlation with the rest of the portfolio. Which bias best fits?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Narrow framing.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Self-attribution.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A stock split.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Semi-strong efficiency.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Narrow framing ignores the broader portfolio context, including correlation and concentration.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An investment's stand-alone appeal does not fully describe its portfolio effect.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>An investor repeatedly dismisses credible evidence against a favored thesis solely because it conflicts with the original view. Which bias is most direct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Portfolio rebalancing.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Belief perseverance.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Currency hedging.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Capital budgeting.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Belief perseverance involves maintaining a prior view while discounting conflicting evidence.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A firm conviction is not a substitute for revising a thesis when facts change.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>An employee puts nearly all savings into their employer's stock because the name feels familiar, without assessing concentration risk. This most directly illustrates:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Interest-rate immunization.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> An ex-dividend adjustment.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Market breadth.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Familiarity bias.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Familiarity can lead an investor to overweight a known company despite concentration risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Familiarity with an employer does not make its stock safe.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>Analysts issue matching recommendations mainly because they fear standing apart from their peers rather than because of independent evidence. This is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A record date.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Dollar-cost averaging.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Herding.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A bond sinking fund.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Herding means following the group, potentially without independent assessment.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Similar recommendations can also result from shared evidence; the question specifies social pressure as the cause.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>Which process most directly reduces the effect of overconfidence and belief perseverance?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Treat all losses as proof of external manipulation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Document the investment thesis in advance, consider disconfirming evidence and periodically review outcomes and costs.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Read only opinions that confirm the existing thesis.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Trade more frequently whenever prices move.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A documented thesis, disconfirming evidence and periodic review can make errors and costs visible.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> More trading or selective evidence can amplify biases.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>Which statement best distinguishes technical from fundamental analysis?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Technical analysis studies price, volume or market patterns; fundamental analysis evaluates economic, industry and business information.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Technical analysis can only use financial statements.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Fundamental analysis ignores company operations by definition.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Both methods guarantee a correct forecast.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Technical analysis uses market behavior such as prices and volume; fundamental analysis studies underlying economic and business factors.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Neither approach guarantees profitable signals.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>On a given day 1,200 stocks advance and 800 decline. What is the day's net advance-decline figure?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> -400</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1,500</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 400</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 2,000</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Net advances = 1,200 - 800 = <strong>400 issues</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> This is a daily difference, not the cumulative line.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>A cumulative advance-decline line ended yesterday at 5,000. Today 1,200 issues advance and 800 decline. What is today's cumulative value?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 5,400</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 5,000</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 4,600</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 7,000</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>New cumulative line = 5,000 + (1,200 - 800) = <strong>5,400</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Add today's net advances to yesterday's cumulative figure.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>The number of stocks reaching 52-week highs exceeds those reaching 52-week lows. Under the chapter's conventional interpretation, this can indicate:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The exact future return of the index.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Broader positive market participation, although it is not a guarantee of future gains.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Proof that prices cannot fall tomorrow.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Proof every stock increased today.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>More new highs than new lows can be read as a positive breadth signal in this framework, but signals can reverse or mislead.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An indicator is not a guarantee about every security.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>There are 1,200 advancing issues, 800 declining issues, 900 million shares of advancing volume and 600 million shares of declining volume. Using TRIN = (advances/declines) / (advancing volume/declining volume), what is TRIN?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.50</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1.50</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 2.25</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>TRIN = $(1{,}200/800)/(900/600)=1.5/1.5=\\mathbf{1.00}$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Compute the ratio of the two ratios, using advancing and declining volume consistently.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>There are 1,200 advancing issues, 800 declining issues, 1,200 million shares of advancing volume and 400 million shares of declining volume. Using the Q25 definition, what is TRIN?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.50</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1.50</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 2.00</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>TRIN = $(1{,}200/800)/(1{,}200/400)=1.5/3=\\mathbf{0.50}$. The slide framework treats lower readings as relatively bullish; a reading is not a certain forecast.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not reverse the volume ratio or treat one signal as proof.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>A fund has RM12 million in cash and RM200 million in total assets under management. Under the chapter's cash/assets convention, what is its mutual fund cash ratio?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 6%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 16.67%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.6%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 60%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>MFCR = cash / assets = RM12m / RM200m = <strong>6%</strong>. The slides interpret higher cash as potential buying capacity, but cash may also meet withdrawals.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A high ratio alone does not predict the market reliably.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>On-balance volume (OBV) begins at 10 million shares. The stock closes higher on volume of 3 million, then closes lower on volume of 2 million. What is the resulting OBV?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 5 million</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 9 million</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 15 million</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 11 million</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>OBV = 10m + 3m - 2m = <strong>11m shares</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Add or subtract the full day's volume according to the close's direction, not just the price change.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>A stock's last three closing prices are RM10, RM12 and RM14. What is its simple three-day moving average?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM14</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM11</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM10</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM12</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Simple moving average = $(10+12+14)/3=\\text{RM}12$. It smooths historical prices but does not guarantee their future direction.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The average is not necessarily the most recent price.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>Short interest rises substantially. Which interpretation best reflects the chapter's competing views?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It guarantees future selling by short sellers to close their positions.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It proves the next market return must be positive.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It can indicate pessimism, while short covering could create future buying demand; neither interpretation guarantees direction.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It measures a company's earnings per share.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>High short interest can reflect pessimism; closing shorts generally requires buying shares, creating potential demand.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The slide's reference to future <em>sales</em> to cover short positions reverses the transaction; covering typically involves buying.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l9p-d3\">PART B - REVISION AND ERROR DIAGNOSIS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l9p-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Quick revision and error diagnosis</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l9_nested\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Each stronger form includes the information of the weaker one. Evidence for a weaker form never proves a stronger one.</figcaption></figure></div><ul>\n<li><strong>EMH:</strong> weak = past trading information; semi-strong = public information; strong = public plus private information. Compare expected and observed returns after accounting for risk and costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Daily breadth</strong> = advances - declines; add this difference to the prior cumulative value to update an advance-decline line.</li>\n<li><strong>TRIN</strong> = (advancing issues / declining issues) ÷ (advancing volume / declining volume); compute only when the relevant denominators are nonzero. <strong>MFCR</strong> = cash / assets under management under the chapter's convention.</li>\n<li><strong>OBV</strong> adds volume on an up-close and subtracts volume on a down-close; under the conventional rule it is unchanged on an unchanged close. A simple moving average is the mean of the specified closing prices.</li>\n<li>Technical indicators, including support/resistance and moving averages, suggest interpretations rather than certain future prices. Test the signal's assumptions, context, sample and costs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Classify mistakes as information-set confusion, bias misidentification, arithmetic, reversed indicator interpretation, or treating a historical association as a guarantee. Ask what information or result would challenge the proposed explanation.</p>\n<p><em>Source note:</em> Original questions based on Chapter 9, <em>Market Efficiency and Behavioral Finance</em> (Pearson slides, 2014). Historical findings and slide trading interpretations are presented as course concepts, not verified current market predictions. Practice timings and targets are illustrative.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"l9-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Difficult mode - 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry practice, CFA-style, case competitions, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the chapter&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"9\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Chapter 9 difficult mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"9\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which item belongs to the <strong>strong-form</strong> information set but <strong>not</strong> to the semi-strong set?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A company's published quarterly earnings release</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Yesterday's closing price and trading volume</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A takeover the board has agreed but not yet announced</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A central bank's interest-rate decision announced this morning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Semi-strong efficiency covers all <strong>public</strong> information, which includes published earnings (A), past market data (B) and public announcements (D). Only the undisclosed takeover is private, so it enters the information set only at the strong form.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which expression is algebraically identical to $\\text{TRIN} = \\dfrac{A/D}{AV/DV}$?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\dfrac{A \\times DV}{D \\times AV}$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\dfrac{A \\times AV}{D \\times DV}$, the issue ratio multiplied by the volume ratio</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\dfrac{A - D}{AV - DV}$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\dfrac{AV/A}{DV/D}$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Dividing by $\\dfrac{AV}{DV}$ is multiplying by $\\dfrac{DV}{AV}$, so $\\text{TRIN} = \\dfrac{A}{D} \\times \\dfrac{DV}{AV}$. B multiplies by the volume ratio instead of dividing; C uses differences, not ratios; D is the <strong>reciprocal</strong> of TRIN (volume per advancing issue over volume per declining issue), so it would reverse the reading.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Under the slides' conventions, which pair of readings is <strong>bullish</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A low mutual fund cash ratio, and odd-lot purchases far exceeding odd-lot sales</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A high mutual fund cash ratio, and odd-lot sales far exceeding odd-lot purchases</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A high TRIN, and declining issues outnumbering advancing issues</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A confidence index falling away from $1$ as the quality spread widens, and more new lows than new highs</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The slides read a high MFCR as cash waiting to buy stocks, and treat odd-lot traders as contrary indicators: when small traders sell heavily, the rule reads bullish. A is the bearish version of both; C and D are both bearish under the slides' readings (high TRIN, negative breadth, a widening quality spread, more new lows).</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Walmart's quarterly revenue peaks every holiday quarter, year after year, but its share price shows no matching seasonal pattern (Figures 9.1 and 9.2). What is the best explanation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Revenue has no effect on the value of a company.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The stock market reacts to earnings only, never to revenue.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Share prices are so noisy from day to day that no piece of company information, predictable or not, can show up in them.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A pattern everyone can predict is already in the price; only departures from the expected seasonal peak are news.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Prices react to surprises. A predictable peak is expected, so it moves expected cash flows before it happens and cannot move the price when it arrives. A and B are false (revenue drives cash flows); C overstates noise: prices do react, but to the unexpected part.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why is a test of market efficiency always a <strong>joint</strong> test?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because it must be run on two markets at the same time.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because \"abnormal\" return is measured against a model of expected return, so a rejection could mean the model is wrong rather than the market.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because both a buyer and a seller are needed for every trade.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because the weak and strong forms must always be tested together on one sample, since the semi-strong form sits between them.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Abnormal return $= R_{\\text{actual}} - E(R)$, and $E(R)$ comes from a risk model. An unexplained return may reflect mispricing, an omitted risk, a flawed benchmark or chance. The other options use \"joint\" in a sense the chapter never means.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Which statement correctly explains the \"future demand\" reading of high short interest?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Every open short position must eventually be closed by buying shares, so short interest is potential future buying.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Short sellers must sell additional shares to close their positions, so a high short interest adds a wave of future supply.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Short interest counts shares bought on margin, which will be bought again later.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> High short interest guarantees a short squeeze within a few weeks.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A short seller borrowed and sold shares, and covers by <strong>buying</strong> them back. B repeats slide 9-27's error (\"sales\" to cover). C confuses short selling with margin buying. D overstates: shorts need not cover together or soon, so no squeeze is guaranteed.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>An advance-decline line stands at $2{,}000$. Over three days: day 1, $1{,}450$ advance and $1{,}050$ decline; day 2, $900$ advance and $1{,}350$ decline; day 3, $1{,}100$ advance and $1{,}100$ decline. What is the line after day 3?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $2{,}400$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $2{,}050$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $1{,}950$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $1{,}550$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Net breadth is $+400$, $-450$ and $0$, so $2{,}000 + 400 - 450 + 0 = \\mathbf{1{,}950}$. A counts only the positive day; D counts only the negative day; B reverses both signs ($2{,}000 - 400 + 450$).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>On one day $1{,}500$ issues advance and $1{,}000$ decline; advancing volume is $720$ million shares and declining volume $640$ million. What is TRIN, and how do the slides read it?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $0.75$: bullish, because more stocks advanced than declined and total volume was heavy</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $1.13$: bearish, because volume rose</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $1.50$: bullish, because the issue ratio is above $1$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $1.33$: bearish, even though more stocks advanced than declined</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>$\\text{TRIN} = \\dfrac{1{,}500/1{,}000}{720/640} = \\dfrac{1.5}{1.125} \\approx \\mathbf{1.33}$. Above $1$, the average declining stock traded more volume ($0.64$m) than the average advancing one ($0.48$m), which the slides read as bearish. A is the reciprocal; B is the volume ratio alone; C is the issue ratio alone.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>On-balance volume stands at $25$ million shares. Over five days the stock closes up on $6$m, down on $4$m, unchanged on $9$m, up on $3$m and down on $7$m. What is OBV now?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $32$ million</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $23$ million</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $14$ million</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $54$ million</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$25 + 6 - 4 + 0 + 3 - 7 = \\mathbf{23}$ million. An unchanged close adds nothing: A wrongly adds the $9$m, C wrongly subtracts it, and D simply adds every volume to the start.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Closes of $\\text{RM}10$, $\\text{RM}11$, $\\text{RM}12$, $\\text{RM}11$ and $\\text{RM}16$ give a five-day SMA of $\\text{RM}12$. The next close is $\\text{RM}13$. Under a rule that signals \"buy\" only when the close <strong>crosses above</strong> its five-day SMA, what happens?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The SMA becomes $\\text{RM}12.60$; no new signal, because the close was already above its average and stays above it.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The SMA becomes $\\text{RM}13.00$; a buy signal fires because the close equals the average.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The SMA becomes $\\text{RM}12.60$; a buy signal fires, because a close above the moving average is by itself the rule's buy condition.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The SMA becomes $\\text{RM}12.17$; a sell signal fires because the price fell from $\\text{RM}16$ to $\\text{RM}13$.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Drop $\\text{RM}10$, add $\\text{RM}13$: $\\dfrac{11 + 12 + 11 + 16 + 13}{5} = \\text{RM}12.60$ (or $12 + \\tfrac{13 - 10}{5}$). Yesterday $\\text{RM}16 &gt; \\text{RM}12$ and today $\\text{RM}13 &gt; \\text{RM}12.60$, so nothing was crossed. C confuses being above with crossing; D averages six closes; B miscomputes the window.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Figure 9.5's NASDAQ column shows $1{,}093$ advancing and $1{,}366$ declining issues, with $808.5$m shares of up volume and $723.5$m of down volume. Net breadth is $-273$ but TRIN is about $0.72$. What does this combination mean?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It is impossible: whenever declining issues outnumber advancing issues, TRIN must come out above $1$, so the data contain an error.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> More stocks rose than fell, and they traded on lower volume.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Fewer stocks rose, but the typical advancing stock traded more heavily than the typical declining one.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The volume figures must have been entered the wrong way round.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$\\text{TRIN} = \\dfrac{1{,}093/1{,}366}{808.5/723.5} = \\dfrac{0.800}{1.117} \\approx 0.72$. Volume per advancing issue is about $0.74$m against $0.53$m per declining issue. Breadth and TRIN measure different things, so they can conflict (A and D are wrong); B misreads the issue counts.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Researchers find that (i) prices fully adjust within a day of public earnings news and (ii) corporate insiders trading in their own firms' shares earn abnormal returns after costs. Which conclusion fits best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> All three forms of efficiency are rejected.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Only the weak form is supported; the semi-strong form is rejected.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The strong form is supported, because insiders are market participants too and their trades are part of how prices are formed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The evidence supports semi-strong (and so weak) efficiency, but challenges the strong form.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Fast adjustment to public news is consistent with semi-strong efficiency, which nests the weak form. Profits from private information contradict only the strong form. A and B ignore (i); C reverses what (ii) implies.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>Strategy X earned $14\\%$ against a risk-model expectation of $11\\%$, with trading costs of $2.4$ points. Strategy Y earned $9\\%$ against an expectation of $7.5\\%$, with costs of $0.6$ point. Which assessment is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> X is better: its raw return is $5$ points higher.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Y shows the larger estimated net abnormal return, $0.9$ point against $0.6$, though both are small enough to be model error or chance.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> X is better: its gross abnormal return is $3$ points against $1.5$, and gross figures are what the risk model is built to measure.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> They are equivalent, because both beat their benchmarks.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Net abnormal return: X $= 14 - 11 - 2.4 = 0.6$; Y $= 9 - 7.5 - 0.6 = 0.9$. A ignores risk; C ignores costs; D ignores the size of the edge. Both estimates remain sensitive to the benchmark and the cost estimate.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>Mutual fund cash rises from $4\\%$ to $9\\%$ of assets. A commentator says this \"guarantees a rally as funds put the cash to work\". Which evaluation is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct: the slides say a high MFCR is bullish, and a jump from $4\\%$ to $9\\%$ is a large build-up that funds must eventually spend on stocks.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Wrong: the slides say a high MFCR is bearish.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Possible buying power, but the cash may be held for expected redemptions or out of caution, with unknown timing; compare with history and mandates.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Irrelevant: fund cash cannot affect stock prices.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The slides read a high MFCR as potentially bullish (so B is wrong), but \"guarantee\" is unjustified: the notes stress that cash can meet withdrawals and need not be invested soon. D overstates in the other direction.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>To \"beat loss aversion\", an investor adopts the rule: sell any stock as soon as it falls $15\\%$ below its purchase price. Which evaluation is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It keeps the decision anchored to the purchase price; reassess prospects at today's price instead, using the fall only as a review trigger.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It is ideal: any mechanical rule removes emotion from investing, and a fixed $15\\%$ limit also caps every possible loss at exactly $15\\%$ of the cost.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It makes loss aversion worse, because selling realises losses.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It is the textbook's recommendation in Table 9.1.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The notes: \"Selling every loser mechanically is no better: the decision should use forward-looking evidence, costs and constraints.\" Table 9.1 asks whether you would buy the stock today with the cash, which is not an automatic rule (D). A rule is not automatically rational (B), and C misstates the bias.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Which design is the most credible test of a strategy that buys after positive earnings surprises?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Backtest on the same sample in which the pattern was first noticed, using gross returns, since that sample is known to contain the effect and so gives the clearest estimate of its size.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Study one well-known company whose price kept rising after good news.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Survey investors about whether they believe in post-earnings drift.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Fix the event time as the first public release, enter at the next executable price, measure returns against a risk-adjusted benchmark after full costs, and test on an independent later sample.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>D follows the notes' checklist: information timing, implementable prices, risk adjustment, costs, out-of-sample evidence. A invites data-mining and ignores costs; B is a single observation; C measures opinion, not returns.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>An investor shows overconfidence, self-attribution and familiarity bias (most savings in the employer's shares). Which set of controls answers those diagnoses best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Trade more often so that feedback arrives faster, rely mainly on the analysts who already agree with the current view, and add to the positions that have worked so far.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Record each forecast with a probability before the result, review all trades against a benchmark after costs, and cap the employer position counting salary exposure.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Sell every losing position immediately and keep every winner.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Move everything into the employer's shares, since the investor knows the company best.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Written forecasts and symmetric reviews target overconfidence and self-attribution; a position cap that counts salary targets familiarity and concentration. A feeds overconfidence and confirmation; C is a mechanical rule unrelated to the diagnoses; D deepens the familiarity problem.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Which specification turns a breadth idea into a <strong>testable</strong> trading rule?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Buy the index at the next day's open when the 10-day average of new highs minus new lows turns positive and the 5-day average TRIN is below $1$; sell when the highs-lows average turns negative; charge $0.1\\%$ per trade; fit on 2000-2015 and test once on 2016-2024.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Buy when the market feels strong and breadth looks healthy, and sell when it does not.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Buy whenever any indicator in Figure 9.5 is bullish.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Buy when a head-and-shoulders pattern appears, as judged by the analyst after the move.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A states the signal, timing, exit, costs and a separate test sample: everything the notes require before a rule can be tested. B is vague, C has no exit and would trade on conflicting signals, and D is judged after the fact.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A fund group holds $\\text{RM}45$m cash out of $\\text{RM}600$m total assets. It then pays $\\text{RM}15$m of redemptions out of cash. What is its mutual fund cash ratio afterwards?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $5.00\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $7.50\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $5.13\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $7.69\\%$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Both cash and total assets fall by $\\text{RM}15$m: $\\dfrac{45 - 15}{600 - 15} = \\dfrac{30}{585} \\approx \\mathbf{5.13\\%}$. A forgets that assets fell too; B is the ratio before redemptions; D keeps the old cash over the new assets. The drop is also a reminder that cash may be held for redemptions, not purchases.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>Around an earnings announcement a stock returns $3.1\\%$, $1.0\\%$ and $0.2\\%$ on days $0$, $1$ and $2$; its risk model expected $0.4\\%$, $-0.3\\%$ and $0.3\\%$. What is the cumulative abnormal return over the three days?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $3.9\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $4.3\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $4.0\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $0.4\\%$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Abnormal returns are $3.1 - 0.4 = 2.7$, $1.0 - (-0.3) = 1.3$ and $0.2 - 0.3 = -0.1$, so $CAR = 2.7 + 1.3 - 0.1 = \\mathbf{3.9\\%}$. B adds the raw returns, C drops the negative day, D adds the expected returns.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>Fractional shares, zero-commission apps and algorithms that slice large institutional orders into small pieces are now common. Why does this weaken the odd-lot contrary rule?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Odd lots are no longer allowed on exchanges.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It makes small traders always right, so the rule simply reverses.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It has no effect: the rule never depended on who trades odd lots, only on the total number of shares traded in lots of fewer than $100$ each day.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Odd-lot volume no longer cleanly tracks small, supposedly mistaken traders, and the rule already assumed such traders are systematically wrong.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The rule infers small-trader sentiment from odd-lot activity. When institutions and every app user trade in odd lots, the proxy blurs. The notes add that changes in trading practices weaken historical interpretations. A and B are false; C ignores the proxy the rule depends on.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>A stock with $\\beta = 1.4$ returned $13\\%$ in a year when the market returned $10\\%$ and the risk-free rate was $3\\%$. Using CAPM as the risk model, what was its abnormal return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $+3.0$ points</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $+0.2$ point</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $-0.2$ point</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $+10.0$ points</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$E(R) = 3\\% + 1.4\\,(10\\% - 3\\%) = 12.8\\%$, so $AR = 13\\% - 12.8\\% = \\mathbf{+0.2}$ point. A compares with the market return and ignores beta; D compares with the risk-free rate; C reverses the sign. Beating the index is not the same as beating the risk-adjusted benchmark.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>A researcher tests $100$ different calendar rules on the same data at a $5\\%$ significance level and finds $6$ \"significant\" ones. What is the best interpretation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> About $100 \\times 5\\% = 5$ false positives are expected even if no rule works; correct for multiple testing and test out of sample.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Six rules are now proven profitable, each at $95\\%$ confidence, so the number of genuine calendar anomalies in the data set is exactly six.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The market is $6\\%$ inefficient.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The other $94$ rules prove the market is efficient.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Finding $6$ when chance alone would produce about $5$ is weak evidence: this is the notes' \"chance after many strategies were tested\" explanation, made numerical. B ignores the number of tests; C and D misread what a significance test measures.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>An anomaly's abnormal return vanishes once a new factor is added to the expected-return model. What can be concluded?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The anomaly was always pure data-mining, because any return that a newly added factor can absorb was never a real pattern in the first place.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Nothing decisive: the factor may be a priced risk or may simply repackage the mispricing (the joint-hypothesis problem).</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Markets are proven efficient.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The new factor must be a behavioral bias.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Abnormal returns are always relative to a model. A new factor that \"explains\" an anomaly could be risk (efficient interpretation) or a description of the pattern (behavioral interpretation). A, C and D each pick one reading the evidence cannot isolate.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>A manager beat a broad index by $3$ points a year for five years with a portfolio of small, illiquid, low-P/E stocks. The pitch deck calls this \"proof of skill in an inefficient market\". Which evaluation is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It is proof of skill: five consecutive years of beating a broad index by $3$ points a year is far too long a record to be explained by luck or by risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It is impossible, so the data must be wrong.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Consistent with skill, but the excess may reward size, value and liquidity exposures the index lacks, or luck; use a benchmark with similar exposures.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Past returns are irrelevant to any evaluation.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The chapter's small-firm and value effects are exactly the anomalies that \"may compensate for risks missing from a model\". Against an index with different risk, the $3$ points is not yet abnormal. A overstates five noisy years; B and D are extreme.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>Which finding would most favour a <strong>behavioral</strong> (under-reaction) explanation of post-earnings-announcement drift over a missing-risk explanation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Drift portfolios have higher betas than the market and lose most heavily in market downturns, so their extra return looks like a reward for bearing risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The drift disappears entirely once trading costs are included.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The drift survives risk adjustment and clusters around the next earnings announcements, when investors are surprised again.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The drift is found only in the single year that was studied.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>If investors under-react, the rest of the news arrives as a fresh surprise at the next announcements; risk compensation would not cluster there. A supports a risk story; B says the pattern is not exploitable but does not explain it; D points to chance.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>If behavioral biases create mispricing, why might it persist even when many rational traders notice it?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Rational traders are legally barred from trading against mispricing.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Mispricing always corrects itself within a single trading day, because so many rational traders are watching that no gap can survive overnight.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Rational traders always agree with biased traders.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Correcting it needs capital and risk: costs, short-sale limits, illiquidity, and the chance that prices first move further away.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>This links the chapter's points about costs, liquidity and short selling to why anomalies may survive. A and C are false; B contradicts the drift evidence the chapter shows.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>Suppose a large review finds that published anomalies earn noticeably less after their publication date than in the original studies' samples, but not zero. Which interpretation is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Partly in-sample overfitting, partly traders exploiting real mispricing once known; published backtests overstate future returns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> All anomalies were pure chance, found by searching through many variables, which is the only reason their returns fell after the studies were published.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> All anomalies are compensation for risk, since returns did not fall to zero.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Publication has no effect on markets.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A partial decline fits both effects the notes list: patterns found by chance shrink on new data, and known anomalies \"disappear once known\" as traders compete. B and C each force a single cause on a partial decline; D contradicts the finding.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>A colleague wants to put all savings, part of which is needed within a year, into a familiar technology stock that has risen for six months and appears to break resistance, citing high short interest as \"proof it must rise\". Which plan is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Invest everything now; momentum, the breakout and short covering all point the same way.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Wait for the stock to fall back below resistance, then invest everything.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Invest everything, but add a stop-loss order a little below the breakout level, which removes the downside risk while keeping all of the upside.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Ring-fence the one-year money in liquid assets; size any stock position within capacity for loss; write the thesis and review triggers.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>D matches the subjective model answer: horizon and liquidity first, then evidence, diversification and written decision rules. A and B rest on familiarity, herding and a two-sided indicator; C forgets that a stop-loss is a trigger, not a guaranteed price (Chapter 3).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>Which research protocol gives the most credible evidence on whether a moving-average crossover rule adds value?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Try many window lengths on the full history, report only the best-performing one, and show its cumulative return against simply holding cash over the period.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Show charts of the rule's best trades from the last decade.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Pre-register window, universe, dates, execution price, costs and benchmark; keep a hold-out period; correct for variants tried; report everything.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Ask experienced traders whether the rule works.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>C addresses specification, implementation, costs, risk, out-of-sample testing and multiple testing. A is classic data-mining, B is selection after the fact (the notes' \"clearly marked buy point\" problem), and D is opinion, not evidence.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
